<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oliver Cromwell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1599">1599</a>–<a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oliver Cromwell was an English soldier and politician who rose to command the New Model Army in the Civil Wars against Charles I and supported the king's execution in 1649. He conquered Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> for the Commonwealth, dissolved the Rump Parliament in 1653 and ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-protector">Lord Protector</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Scotland and Ireland under the Instrument of Government until his death in 1658.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell.jpg" alt="Oliver Cromwell" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Samuel Cooper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oliver_Cromwell_by_Samuel_Cooper.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The two had shared a monarch since 1603, when James VI of Scotland succeeded Elizabeth I as James I of England, but they kept separate parliaments, churches, laws and trade, and a union imposed under <b>Oliver Cromwell</b> had lapsed at the Restoration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The rising of 1641 began a decade of war involving the Catholic Confederation of Kilkenny, royalists, Scottish Covenanters and English parliamentarians, ended by <b>Oliver Cromwell</b>’s campaign of 1649–1650, with the storming of Drogheda and Wexford, and the completion of the conquest by 1653.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1640s the Covenanting army was among the strongest in Britain, but <b>Oliver Cromwell</b> crushed Scottish forces at Dunbar in 1650 and Worcester in 1651.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Oliver Cromwell</b>’s Protectorate of 1653–1658 was described as a military dictatorship by his enemies and by some modern scholars, and Maximilien Robespierre was called a dictator while the Committee of Public Safety directed the Reign of Terror in France in 1793–1794.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Its General Council debated the constitution at Putney in 1647, and in 1653 soldiers expelled the Rump Parliament and army officers drafted the Instrument of Government that made <b>Oliver Cromwell</b> Lord Protector.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Montesquieu and Rousseau thought republics workable only in small states, and the Commonwealth of England, which ended in <b>Oliver Cromwell</b>’s personal rule as Lord Protector, persuaded many that republics led to anarchy or tyranny.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huntingdon, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Palace of Whitehall, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.34,-0.17&amp;year=1637" aria-label="Huntingdon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4546 357 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Oliver Cromwell"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4996 627h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4996 627h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.34,-0.17&amp;year=1637">What was here in 1637? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I had rather have a plain russet-coated Captain that knows what he fights for, and loves what he knows, than that which you call ‘a Gentleman’ and is nothing else.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Suffolk committee, 29 August 1643 &#183; original &#183; from Carlyle's edition of his letters and speeches (1845) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Works_of_Thomas_Carlyle/Volume_6/Letter_16" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I beseech you be careful what Captains of Horse you choose, what men be mounted: a few honest men are better than numbers.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Suffolk committee, 29 August 1643 &#183; original &#183; from Carlyle's edition of his letters and speeches (1845) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Works_of_Thomas_Carlyle/Volume_6/Letter_16" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Liberty of conscience, and liberty of the subject,—two as glorious things to be contended for as any that God hath given us…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the opening of the first Protectorate Parliament, 4 September 1654 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/At_the_Opening_of_Parliament_Under_the_Protectorate" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What I judge to be the end of your meeting, the great end, which was likewise remembered to you this day; to wit, is healing and settling.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the opening of the first Protectorate Parliament, 4 September 1654 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/At_the_Opening_of_Parliament_Under_the_Protectorate" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
